# Plain English — no jargon

## Rule

In **chat** and **`docs/INSTRUCTIONS.md`**, do **not** use jargon.

Write like you are explaining to someone who has never opened the findings folder or a disc tool.

## Use

- Everyday words: movie, cutscene, disc image, fixed place on the disc, jump in the script, copy over, break, fix, test, rebuild.
- What the player or burner sees: which scene, what goes wrong, what to click or run.
- Exact file paths and shell commands when the human must run them (paths are not “explaining”).

## Do not use (in chat / INSTRUCTIONS)

Technical nicknames, engine opcodes, disc-format labels, and research shorthand as wording, including but not limited to short codes and lab terms for disc layout, movie tables, script ops, and inject steps.

Say what happened instead of naming the mechanism:

| Do not write | Write |
|--------------|--------|
| fixed address seek for the lake movie | the lake cutscene always reads one fixed place on the disc |
| script jump skips the play-movie op | a jump in the map script skips that movie on purpose |
| put lake movie bytes back on that spot | we put the real lake movie back on top of that part of the disc |
| hole in the long credits stream | a stretch of the long credits is overwritten by another movie |
| speedrun movie pack required files | the few movies the speedrun pack must keep on the disc |

## Allowed elsewhere

Code, script names, tool-facing commits, and `docs/findings/` may use precise terms.

**Chat and INSTRUCTIONS:** plain words only. If a path or command contains a technical filename, keep the filename; do not decorate the explanation with more technical talk.

## Fail test

Before sending: if a non-modder would need a glossary, rewrite.
